package htmxui

// UserMenuData carries UserMenu's identity + logout parameterisation (FR8).
//
// Both fields are plain strings, not a //libs/go/htmxauth dependency: the
// component only ever needs a label to display and an href to link to, and
// taking htmxauth.UserInfo here would give htmxui a dependency on a specific
// auth implementation for no behavioural gain -- a caller with a different
// auth story (or a synthetic/test identity) can populate UserMenuData
// without htmxui needing to know what a UserInfo is. This mirrors
// ShellData.UserLabel's existing plain-string choice (shell.templ) for the
// same reason.
//
// LogoutHref is a parameter, not a hardcoded "/auth/logout" constant: both
// current apps register that same route (htmxauth.Authenticator.HandleLogout),
// but UserMenu must not assume every caller mounts logout at that path.
// htmxui_test.go's TestUserMenu_LogoutHrefIsParameterised guards this
// directly by rendering with a non-default href and asserting the default
// path is absent from the output.
type UserMenuData struct {
	// IdentityLabel is the signed-in user's display text, e.g. a
	// PreferredUsername or Name claim. Empty means "no user" -- UserMenu
	// renders nothing in that case (see UserMenu's doc comment for why).
	IdentityLabel string
	// LogoutHref is the link target for the logout affordance.
	LogoutHref string
}

// UserMenu is htmxui's shared top-right identity + logout component (FR8).
// Both tools/app_registry and manmanv2 build their top-right chrome from
// this single implementation instead of each maintaining their own:
// app-registry's tools/app_registry/ui/components/layout.templ Shell()
// today renders only `<span>{ user.PreferredUsername }</span>` -- identity
// with no logout control at all -- and manmanv2's
// manmanv2/ui/components/layout.templ Nav() renders
// `Logout ({ data.User.Name })` as a bare link, duplicated a second time
// for its mobile menu. UserMenu covers both: it always shows identity
// alongside a visible logout affordance.
//
// Signed-out / no-user state: when data.IdentityLabel is empty, UserMenu
// renders nothing (no dropdown, no stray logout link to nowhere). This
// matters for AUTH_MODE=none, where callers may have no real user to
// display -- rendering an empty-but-present menu would offer a logout
// control with no identity behind it, which is more confusing than
// omitting the control entirely. A caller with a real signed-out state that
// wants a login affordance instead composes one alongside UserMenu via the
// HeaderRight slot (see below) -- UserMenu itself only ever speaks to the
// signed-in case, matching Shell's existing UserLabel convention of "empty
// renders nothing" (shell.templ).
//
// Composition, not folding (FR8): UserMenu takes no app-specific
// parameters, and app-specific extras -- e.g. manmanv2's ServerSelector --
// are never passed into it. Instead, a caller composes UserMenu and its own
// extras together into a single templ.Component and passes that combined
// component as ShellData.HeaderRight (shell.templ); Shell renders
// HeaderRight verbatim, so UserMenu and the caller's extras render as
// siblings in that slot, not nested inside one another.
// htmxui_test.go's TestUserMenu_ComposesAlongsideHeaderRightSlot renders
// exactly that shape (a marker component plus UserMenu, both passed as one
// HeaderRight component to Shell) and asserts the marker's markup falls
// outside UserMenu's own dropdown container rather than inside it.
//
// Mobile/narrow presentation: UserMenu renders a single daisyUI
// `dropdown`/`menu` control (mirroring ThemeSwitcher's dropdown pattern in
// theme_switcher.templ) rather than manmanv2's current approach of a
// visible-at-desktop-width inline link plus a second, separately-marked-up
// copy for its mobile hamburger panel. A collapsed button that expands a
// dropdown on tap/click needs no width-specific variant to remain usable at
// narrow viewports, so one implementation covers both cases; this is a
// deliberate simplification over manmanv2's current two-copies-of-the-same-
// link approach, not an oversight -- there is no separate
// MobileUserMenu here, and adopting apps should not maintain their own
// mobile duplicate of this control either.
templ UserMenu(data UserMenuData) {
	if data.IdentityLabel != "" {
		<div class="dropdown dropdown-end" data-htmxui-user-menu>
			<div tabindex="0" role="button" class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm" aria-label="Account menu">
				{ data.IdentityLabel }
			</div>
			<ul tabindex="0" class="dropdown-content menu bg-base-100 text-base-content rounded-box z-10 w-40 p-2 shadow">
				<li><a href={ templ.URL(data.LogoutHref) }>Logout</a></li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	}
}
